Withdrawing a **Hidden** group publishes a public discovery card carrying its name, description, tags, owner agent id and member count. A group whose entire purpose is being undiscoverable becomes discoverable at the moment it is deleted.

This is exploitable today and is independent of #449 — I found it while designing that fix (recorded there as D6), but it needs no part of it.

## Mechanism

Two conditions line up, each of which reads as correct on its own.

`maybe_publish_group_card_after_state_change` (`src/server/routes/named_groups.rs:4694-4697`):

```rust
let discoverable = info.withdrawn
    || info.policy.discoverability != x0x::groups::GroupDiscoverability::Hidden;
if discoverable {
    publish_group_card_to_discovery(state, group_id).await;
}
```

`GroupInfo::to_group_card` (`src/groups/mod.rs:2247-2250`):

```rust
pub fn to_group_card(&self) -> Option<GroupCard> {
    if self.policy.discoverability == GroupDiscoverability::Hidden && !self.withdrawn {
        return None;
    }
```

So `withdrawn` overrides `Hidden` in **both** places. While a Hidden group is live it produces no card; the moment it is withdrawn it produces one and that card is published to public discovery.

## What leaks

`to_group_card` (`groups/mod.rs:2251+`) populates `name`, `description`, `tags`, `policy_summary`, `owner_agent_id`, `member_count`, `request_access_enabled`. For an ADR-0038 Home that is the owner's space name (renamable, so plausibly personal), its description, the owner's age

## Problem

The #184 mitigation retains the latest authenticated `AgentId -> MachineId` binding learned from an accepted verified `IdentityAnnouncement` and rejects gossip-DM envelope claims that disagree with it. This materially blocks the demonstrated Trusted/non-Pinned bypass when a prior binding exists, but it cannot authenticate the physical origin machine for a pure relayed DM when no binding is available.

The residual occurs for:

- a Trusted contact whose live identity announcement has never been observed;
- process restart, before announcements repopulate the in-memory binding cache;
- bounded-LRU eviction of a retained binding.

In those states the receiver can only check the sender-agent-signed `sender_machine_id` claim. A holder of the agent key can claim an unrevoked machine and bypass a revocation of the actual origin machine. `AgentCertificate` is not a solution: it binds user to agent, not agent to machine.

## Required protocol design

Bind a fresh machine-key attestation into each gossip DM (or a cryptographically linked short-lived attestation referenced by it) so the receiver can verify that the claimed origin machine key authorized this specific DM context. The signed material must cover at least:

- sender `AgentId`;
- sender `MachineId` / machine public key identity;
- recipient `AgentId`;
- DM request ID and freshness/expiry fields;
- a domain separator and protocol version.

